<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Surakarta Sunanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1755 CE – 1946 CE · capital Surakarta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Senior successor of the Javanese Mataram dynasty, whose Susuhunan kept an influential court at Surakarta until the revolution of 1946.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Chinese War sacked the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a> in 1742, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-ii">Susuhunan Pakubuwono II</a> moved the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram</a> capital to Surakarta in 1745. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti">Treaty of Giyanti</a> in 1755 divided the realm, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-iii">Pakubuwono III</a> with the eastern half while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yogyakarta-sultanate">Yogyakarta Sultanate</a> took the west, and in 1757 further lands went to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mangkunegaran">Mangkunegaran</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Susuhunan presided over the most elaborate court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, regarded as the guardian of Javanese tradition, with royal poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranggawarsita">Ronggowarsito</a>, gamelan ensembles, bedhaya dances and Islamic ritual. Real power rested increasingly with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> resident, while royal lands were leased to European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and tobacco planters in the 19th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Unlike <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yogyakarta-sultanate">Yogyakarta</a>, the court did not win popular support during the Indonesian revolution. An anti-royalist movement in 1946 led the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Republic of Indonesia</a> to end its special status, and Surakarta became an ordinary city. The kraton survives with a ceremonial Susuhunan.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/surakarta-sunanate.jpg" alt="Surakarta Sunanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Lyndonbaines · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sunanate_of_Surakarta.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1755 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Surakarta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pakubuwono II, Pakubuwono III, Pakubuwono X</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kasunanan Surakarta, Surakarta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1850&amp;focus=surakarta-sunanate" aria-label="Surakarta Sunanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7745 2460 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Surakarta Sunanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8065 2716L8052 2725M8065 2716L7959 2663M8065 2716L7959 2663M8065 2716L7959 2664M8065 2716L8065 2716"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8052 2725h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0M8065 2716h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8065 2716h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8065 2716h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Surakarta. <a href="/atlas?year=1850&amp;focus=surakarta-sunanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
